1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,143
A friend hands you a coin, and swears that it's fair.

2
00:00:03,533 --> 00:00:05,922
To check, you flip it 100 times.

3
00:00:06,233 --> 00:00:08,473
It lands heads 62 times.

4
00:00:09,633 --> 00:00:10,956
Is that suspicious?

5
00:00:11,276 --> 00:00:13,793
Or is it just the kind of luck you'd expect?

6
00:00:14,183 --> 00:00:18,002
The tool statisticians reach for here is called a p-value.

7
00:00:18,322 --> 00:00:23,079
It's one of the most used, and most misunderstood, numbers in all of science.

8
00:00:24,917 --> 00:00:26,069
Here's the trick.

9
00:00:26,389 --> 00:00:30,720
Instead of trying to prove the coin is biased, we play devil's advocate.

10
00:00:31,117 --> 00:00:38,114
We assume the boring explanation: the coin really is fair, with exactly a 50% chance of heads.

11
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:43,321
This boring assumption has a name: the null hypothesis, written H₀.

12
00:00:43,967 --> 00:00:45,759
Then we ask one question.

13
00:00:46,079 --> 00:00:50,474
If the null hypothesis were true, how surprising would our data be?

14
00:00:52,317 --> 00:00:59,570
To find out, imagine running the whole experiment again and again, in a world where the coin is perfectly fair.

15
00:00:59,817 --> 00:01:03,401
Each run, we flip 100 times and count the heads.

16
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:04,659
53.

17
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:07,388
48.

18
00:01:09,033 --> 00:01:10,036
51.

19
00:01:10,233 --> 00:01:13,646
Stack up thousands of these runs, and a shape emerges.

20
00:01:18,133 --> 00:01:20,288
Most runs land near 50 heads.

21
00:01:20,608 --> 00:01:23,616
Results far from 50 get rarer and rarer.

22
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,005
This is the null distribution.

23
00:01:26,325 --> 00:01:29,034
It's a picture of what pure chance looks like.

24
00:01:30,683 --> 00:01:32,902
Now, where does our 62 fall?

25
00:01:33,049 --> 00:01:34,820
Way out here, in the tail.

26
00:01:34,966 --> 00:01:41,899
The p-value is the fraction of this fair-coin world that produces a result at least as extreme as ours.

27
00:01:42,283 --> 00:01:46,912
62 or more heads happens only about 1% of the time.

28
00:01:47,299 --> 00:01:55,875
But a coin biased toward tails would be just as suspicious, so we also count the mirror-image tail: 38 heads or fewer.

29
00:01:56,266 --> 00:01:58,186
Together, that's about 2%.

30
00:01:58,506 --> 00:02:01,173
Our p-value is 0.02.

31
00:02:03,017 --> 00:02:05,257
So here's the definition, in full.

32
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:13,869
The p-value is the probability of seeing data at least as extreme as what you observed, assuming the null hypothesis is true.

33
00:02:14,267 --> 00:02:16,379
Notice that little vertical bar.

34
00:02:16,699 --> 00:02:21,158
It means everything is computed inside the world where H₀ holds.

35
00:02:21,478 --> 00:02:25,531
Keep an eye on it, because it's the source of most of the confusion.

36
00:02:27,367 --> 00:02:33,063
In practice, people choose a cutoff in advance, called alpha, usually 0.05.

37
00:02:33,450 --> 00:02:41,813
If the p-value falls below alpha, the result is called statistically significant, and we reject the null hypothesis.

38
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:45,315
Watch what happens as we slide the number of heads.

39
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:51,653
At 60 heads, the p-value is about 0.057.

40
00:02:51,973 --> 00:02:53,317
Not significant.

41
00:02:53,717 --> 00:02:56,746
At 61, it drops to 0.035.

42
00:02:57,066 --> 00:02:58,218
Significant.

43
00:02:58,617 --> 00:03:00,750
One extra head flips the verdict.

44
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:05,294
The line at 0.05 is a convention, not a law of nature.

45
00:03:07,133 --> 00:03:08,477
Now for the traps.

46
00:03:08,616 --> 00:03:09,917
Trap number one.

47
00:03:10,237 --> 00:03:16,552
A p-value of 0.02 does not mean there's a 2% chance the coin is fair.

48
00:03:16,949 --> 00:03:21,472
The p-value is the probability of the data, given the hypothesis.

49
00:03:21,792 --> 00:03:25,376
Not the probability of the hypothesis, given the data.

50
00:03:25,766 --> 00:03:29,009
And flipping a conditional can change it enormously.

51
00:03:29,149 --> 00:03:34,674
The chance that someone speaks English, given they're a U.S. president, is nearly 1.

52
00:03:35,066 --> 00:03:41,125
But the chance that someone is a U.S. president, given they speak English, is almost zero.

53
00:03:42,967 --> 00:03:44,268
Trap number two.

54
00:03:44,588 --> 00:03:47,511
A small p-value does not mean a big effect.

55
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:51,697
Picture a coin that lands heads 51% of the time.

56
00:03:52,017 --> 00:03:58,012
Flip it 100 times, get 51 heads, and the p-value is about 0.92.

57
00:03:58,332 --> 00:03:59,463
Nothing to see.

58
00:03:59,850 --> 00:04:06,997
But flip it 10,000 times, get 5,100 heads, and the p-value drops below 0.05.

59
00:04:07,233 --> 00:04:08,684
Same tiny bias.

60
00:04:09,004 --> 00:04:15,767
With more data, the null distribution gets narrower, so even a small deviation ends up in the tail.

61
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:18,880
Trap number three.

62
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:23,040
A large p-value does not prove the null hypothesis.

63
00:04:23,433 --> 00:04:26,569
It only means the data aren't surprising under it.

64
00:04:26,716 --> 00:04:28,231
Maybe the coin is fair.

65
00:04:28,551 --> 00:04:32,882
Or maybe it's slightly biased, and we just didn't flip it enough to tell.

66
00:04:33,266 --> 00:04:35,847
The honest conclusion is: undetermined.

67
00:04:37,883 --> 00:04:39,248
Trap number four.

68
00:04:39,568 --> 00:04:47,803
Even when every null hypothesis is true, p-values below 0.05 still show up, about one time in twenty.

69
00:04:48,199 --> 00:04:54,983
Test 20 perfectly fair coins, and there's a 64% chance at least one of them looks significant.

70
00:04:55,383 --> 00:04:59,159
Run enough tests, and chance alone will hand you a discovery.

71
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:03,043
So, what is a p-value?

72
00:05:03,183 --> 00:05:04,996
It's a measure of surprise.

73
00:05:05,316 --> 00:05:10,223
It tells you how strange your data would look if the boring explanation were true.

74
00:05:10,616 --> 00:05:14,413
A small p-value means chance alone would rarely do this.

75
00:05:14,733 --> 00:05:18,104
That's a reason to look closer, not a final verdict.
